#{[17592186371250 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371250 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371250 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371250 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=5{x}^{2}-4x+5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371250 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d5d66cf-660f-56ca-bb31-77faaca8db97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371250 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3\\pm \\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +6(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+10(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n10\\alpha {x}^{2}+(12\\alpha +10\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +6\\beta +10\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}10\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 12\\alpha +10\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +6\\beta +10\\gamma =5\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =-1,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by \n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371251 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371251 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371251 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371251 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371251 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371251 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371251 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371251 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371251 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a7d23e8b-11b4-5ec1-a02e-b04b713d3e20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371252 #edb/ref 1080 "y={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371252 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371252 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "98457a6f-2eb2-5f65