8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432548 #edb/ref 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+3\\alpha =-15\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432549 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432549 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432549 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432549 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=4x+1\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-x}+x-1,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=4x+1,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ad0a4da-a37e-5e52-a84f-310fd3db4009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432549 #edb/ref 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B-1 & =1 \\\\ A+B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4x}-B{e}^{-x}+1,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-1$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4A-B+1 & =-1 \\\\ -4A-B & =-2 \\\\ 4A+B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=2 \\\\ 4A+B=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,B=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-x}+x-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432550 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432550 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432550 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432550 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-y=-{x}^{2}-3x+4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432550 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b4d62141-8603-59c2-87a3-6eb4ab644ea2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432550 #edb/ref 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-3x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-\\alpha {x}^{2}-\\beta x+(2\\alpha -\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-3x+4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ -\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -\\gamma =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =3,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432551 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432551 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432551 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432551 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=18x+3,\\,y(0)=3,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432551 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bcffa98b-601b-574f-8230-ffea834e6b94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432551 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =2$ or $\\lambda =3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(-5\\alpha +6\\beta )=18x+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =18\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -5\\alpha +6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x+3.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+3x+3.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=0.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2A{e}^{2x}+3B{e}^{3x}+3\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n2A+3B+3=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ 2A+3B+3=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=3,B=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{2x}-3{e}^{3x}+3x+3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432552 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432552 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432552 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432552 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4y=-12\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432552 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ffc3a9b8-ace7-50b5-8eec-e5287c7960c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432552 #edb/ref 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+4\\alpha =-12\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432553 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432553 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93784" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432553 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432553 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+20y=18-10x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432553 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ffc50f3b-27ee-5817-a670-1155673ba640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432553 #edb/ref 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+4\\alpha +20(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18-10x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n20\\alpha x+(4\\alpha +20\\beta )=18-10x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}20\\alpha =-10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 4\\alpha +20\\beta =18\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{x}{2}+1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
